Fabric executor and handler exception boundaries
===================================

Caught native-operation, target-lookup, artifact-persistence, staging and plugin
exceptions are normalized without copying their text or traceback into an
OperationResult or the executor's logs. Such exceptions may contain database
parameters, connection URLs, credentials or private payloads. Operation IDs and
fixed failure categories remain available for diagnosis. Existing error codes
and the API's failed-operation mapping are preserved.

The change does not retry an operation, change authorization, auto-apply a staged
write or promise rollback of an external effect. Artifact cleanup after a failed
stage is still attempted under the same organization. Cancellation propagates.
Successful outputs and explicit OperationResult.fail messages returned by a
handler are preserved; each handler remains responsible for making its own
outputs and declared errors safe. The three VPN status/peer/route handlers and
four VoIP status/call/extension/origination handlers now return fixed errors for
caught service exceptions, including construction failures. Their existing error
codes, invocation scope, successful response shapes and cancellation behavior
remain intact. Built-in notifications and webhooks also normalize dispatch,
transport and SSRF/URL exceptions without their text or traceback; notification
failure logs keep only a fixed message. Successful webhook response bodies are
still intentional output, not sanitized by this change. A failed origination response does not prove that the external
call had no effect; operators must verify PBX state before retrying. Other services' logging and the contents of
historical run records are outside this bounded correction.

The regression suite uses synthetic canary details and mocked faulting services.
It checks both serialized results and captured logs, staging call counts, artifact
cleanup, the invocation endpoint's error bridge, success/refusal controls and
cancellation. It does not contact hardware or simulate production credentials.
